- How would you benchmark throughput under 1000 producers vs 1 consumer?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design crash-consistent data structures for persistent memory, requiring reasoning about durability guarantees, lock-free synchronization patterns, and recovery mechanisms after power loss.

## Key Concepts

- **Crash-consistency in PMEM**: Using CLWB (Cache Line Write Back) to flush modified cache lines to persistent media without invalidating, combined with SFENCE to enforce memory ordering and guarantee durability
- **Lock-free MPSC patterns**: Utilizing atomic operations on persistent indices to enable multiple producers to enqueue concurrently without locks
- **Recovery mechanisms**: Detecting and repairing partially written slots, reconstructing in-flight state after system restart

## Data Structures

```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t seq;
    uint8_t payload[56];
} slot_t;

typedef struct {
    volatile uint64_t head;
    volatile uint64_t tail;
} pmem_queue_t;
```

Each slot is 64 bytes (cache-line aligned), with the sequence number serving as both a validity marker and a position indicator. The head and tail indices are stored in persistent memory to survive power failures.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of NUMA-aware memory management and lock-free structures beyond simple queues. Candidates must justify locality decisions, reclamation delay trade-offs, and fragmentation management under mixed workloads. The prompt also probes API design, minimal synchronization strategies, and practical benchmarking methodology.

## Key Concepts

- **NUMA locality**: Allocations should prefer the local node to avoid expensive remote memory access
- **Slab allocator design**: Pre-allocates memory in fixed-size slabs per object size class
- **Size bucketing**: Groups objects into size classes (32–128B, 1–4KB) to reduce fragmentation
- **Lock-free free lists**: CAS-based singly-linked lists enable concurrent access without heavy locking
- **Cross-node reclamation**: Deferred reclamation via epoch orquEUe avoids false sharing and reduces ping-ponging
- **Alignment and false sharing**: Cache-line padding prevents unrelated objects from sharing the same line
- **TLB/cache effects**: Slab locality improves cache hit rates; large pages (huge TLB entries) reduce TLB misses for bulk operations

## Code Example

